The concurrency contract, priced. An encode context is not thread safe (ABI v1
section 3) and the rust slice's positive control found that sharing one ABORTS
the process rather than producing wrong bytes. `[ThreadStatic]` is how a managed
host satisfies that without a lock, and what it costs is one context per thread
the POOL decides to use, not per call:

  encode contexts created:          7
  decode contexts created:          7
  native staging they hold:         7,370,720 bytes
  concurrency levels driven:        1, 8, 16
  processors:                       4

The count is a property of the THREAD POOL, not of the call rate: a context
is created the first time the pool runs a marshaller on a thread that has not
run one, and it is never freed. An ArmoniK worker with a large pool therefore
holds a staging buffer per pool thread, sized from the LARGEST payload that
thread ever encoded. That is the cost of the lock-free answer, and it is the
argument for a pooled context with a try-lock rather than a thread-local one.

